                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "There is so much to say about this game, so to not repeat most of the other reviews, I want to talk about immersion a little bit. Bear with me. Context.\nI play games to get immersed. If a game does not do that for me then I usually end up just forgetting about it and playing something else. What it takes for me to get immersed in a game is challenge, meaningful consequence, a game has to demand all my attention through it's gameplay. Fancy graphics are largely irrelevant as long as they don't get in the way. This means permadeath and iron-man, hardcore survival, complex and rich crafting, deep tactics and strategy, complicated logistics. \nSo, think The Long Dark, Kenshi, Haven and Hearth, Project Zomboid, Empyrion, Valheim, Songs of Syx, Stationeers, Dwarf Fortress, Sailwind, Unreal World, Factorio, Plains of Pain, Vein, Battle Brothers, From the Depths, Rain World, CDDA... Yadda yadda... Immersion.\nRising World was released to Steam in 2014, is and has been continuously developed In all that time and I had never heard of it. The algorithm didn't bring it to my attention, never noticed it mentioned on the forums I frequent, and I guess this makes sense given my above mentioned preferences. \nDunno why I bought it now. No Idea. On the face of it, it looks like a pass. It looks casual and timid. It looks like the opposite of immersive. \nThis game has me properly immersed. \nI could say it is the graphics, the controls, the audio, the crafting, the world, the survival, but it is none of those things. In fact, I cannot even particularly praise any of these things.\nIt is all of them and more. But I can't really say why this adds up to being one of the most immersive games I have played. \nMy first experience of it was, I started playing, chop trees, break stone, make tools blah blah, and just kept playing, a few hours later I was lost in the wilderness. I had survived an attack by wolves by first of all trying fighting, realising I had no chance, running, realising I had no chance, happening across a lake and swimming to safety, nearly freezing to death in the cold water.... crawling up the bank. The wolves, frustrated, gave up.\nCouldn't find my way back to my base (just a very basic starter shelter and campfire and workbench) so I started walking. And walking. And walking. Enjoying the scenery not noticing the passing of time. Found another nice spot for a base a couple of (in-game) days later, gathered some mats, settled in a bit got iron-working going, a thunder storm comes up and I'm watching the lightning over the mountains when there is a strike near where I am standing. I nearly jumped out of my seat. As the wind and rain started I ran for the shelter of my cave. This was not a considered action it was pure immersion. I was there. And this is emergent. From procedural generation. This world is incredibly well done procgen.\nThis is something this game succeeds at. The dev just has the knack. It is talent or skill or just knowing what they want and doing just that. It all works. \nIt is possible to criticise the technicalities, the combat, the  movement, the graphics, the audio... but how can I criticise the parts when they all add up to this unique immersive experience?\nA game that is not particularly challenging, the survival mechanics are not brutal. It's almost, dare I say it, casual. However, it is clearly a work of incredible talent and determined focus. And that makes it outstanding.\n10/10 \nThe definition of hidden gem.\nCan I say this is going to be your experience? I dunno why it is even my experience. Glad I discovered it though.\nRuns perfectly on my linux laptop. Lots of options to customise for performance and gameplay preference.",
